# Query workflows

Query is server-state coordination. It does not choose HTTP, RPC, SQL, local
storage, persistence, or a widget tree for the application.

## Name cache identity first

```dart
final listKey = QueryKey('posts.list', <Object?>['newest']);
final detailKey = QueryKey('posts.detail', <Object?>[42]);
```

Keys snapshot JSON-like parts, including nested lists and string-keyed maps.
Prefer a small scalar identity. Use structured parts only when they really
distinguish resources.

## Connect typed RPC with cancellation

The full-stack product uses its generated record reference directly. Create the
options once in the list widget's `State`, then replace them only when the sort
input or captured RPC client actually changes:

```dart
late QueryOptions<List<Post>> _listOptions;

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  _listOptions = _createListOptions();
}

@override
void didUpdateWidget(PostsPage oldWidget) {
  super.didUpdateWidget(oldWidget);
  if (oldWidget.sort != widget.sort ||
      !identical(oldWidget.rpc, widget.rpc)) {
    _listOptions = _createListOptions();
  }
}

QueryOptions<List<Post>> _createListOptions() {
  final sort = widget.sort;
  final rpc = widget.rpc;
  return QueryOptions<List<Post>>(
    key: QueryKey('posts.list', <Object?>[sort]),
    query: (query) => routes.listPosts(
      rpc,
      sort,
      cancelled: query.cancelToken.whenCancelled.then<void>((_) {}),
    ),
  );
}
```

Reading the cancel token marks the fetch as cancellation-aware. Removing the
last observer or explicitly cancelling the query can then abort
`HttpTransport`. RPC reports `RpcCancelledException`; Query keeps normal
cancellation out of error state.

## Render every useful state

Keep stale data visible during a background refresh, but make an initial error
actionable:

```dart
QueryBuilder<List<Post>>(
  options: _listOptions,
  builder: (_, result) {
    if (!result.hasData && result.isFetching) {
      return const CircularProgressIndicator();
    }
    if (result.isError && !result.hasData) {
      return FilledButton(
        onPressed: () => client.invalidateQueries(
          QueryFilter(key: _listOptions.key, exact: true),
        ),
        child: const Text('Retry'),
      );
    }
    return ListView(
      children: <Widget>[
        for (final post in result.requireData)
          ListTile(title: Text(post.title)),
      ],
    );
  },
)
```

An exact filter targets only this resource. A prefix filter is appropriate
after a mutation that invalidates a collection and its members together.

When a background refresh fails and cached data still exists, keep that stale
data visible but show that it may be out of date and provide a retry. Do not
silently clear a successful mutation input while presenting an unchanged list
as fresh server truth.

## Keep mutations and queries separate

Mutation results have their own cache, observers, retry policy, and scope. The
starter sends `CreatePost` and receives the complete inserted `Post` from one
typed `INSERT ... RETURNING` statement. The form widget's `State` owns one
reusable mutation options instance:

```dart
final _postListsKey = QueryKey('posts.list');
late MutationOptions<Post, CreatePost, void> _createOptions;

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  _createOptions = _createMutationOptions();
}

@override
void didUpdateWidget(CreatePostForm oldWidget) {
  super.didUpdateWidget(oldWidget);
  if (!identical(oldWidget.rpc, widget.rpc)) {
    _createOptions = _createMutationOptions();
  }
}

MutationOptions<Post, CreatePost, void> _createMutationOptions() {
  final rpc = widget.rpc;
  return MutationOptions<Post, CreatePost, void>(
    key: QueryKey('posts.create'),
    mutation: (input, _) => routes.createPost(rpc, input),
    onSuccess: (_, _, _, mutation) => mutation.client.invalidateQueries(
      QueryFilter(key: _postListsKey),
    ),
  );
}

@override
Widget build(BuildContext context) =>
    MutationBuilder<Post, CreatePost, void>(
      options: _createOptions,
      builder: (_, state, mutate, _) => FilledButton(
        onPressed: state.isPending
            ? null
            : () => mutate((title: titleController.text)),
        child: Text(state.isPending ? 'Creating…' : 'Create'),
      ),
    );
```

Options identity belongs to the observer lifecycle. Recreating query options
inside `build` reconfigures the query observer and may refetch stale data.
Mutation options update the definition used by the next execution while an
active execution and its state stay attached. Reuse stored instances for
unrelated rebuilds; replace them only when a real input, captured client, or
operation policy changes.

The prefix filter invalidates every sorted post list while leaving unrelated
resources alone. The UI disables duplicate submission, waits for invalidation,
and clears the input only after success. It does not optimistically invent a
database ID.

After any successful side effect, invalidate only the queries whose server
truth may have changed. Cache removal cannot interrupt a mutation Future that
already started and cannot roll back its side effects.

When offline retry is enabled, a paused mutation waits for the online manager.
`QueryClient.clear()` cancels a mutation before its next attempt and releases
listeners, but still does not revoke completed external work.

## Share initial server state deliberately

When Query and Document are installed for one server request, Document can
serialize completed and pending Query values into the handoff. Flutter restores
them before taking over. Use the same `Serializer` adapter set on the server,
`RpcModule`, and `DocumentModule`; a custom wire tag available on only one side
is rejected.

Persistence is another explicit adapter. Installing Query never silently
writes cache state to disk.

See [Query](/docs/concepts/query) for the complete lifecycle and
[Server and RPC](/docs/concepts/server-rpc) for the transport boundary.
